WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.156
This briefing comes from
Book 04 of the Federal

2
00:00:03.156 --> 00:00:06.263
Modernization Workbook,
Access and Privilege.

3
00:00:06.263 --> 00:00:08.750
Book 02 moved the identity.

4
00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:11.788
Book 03 made its facts trustworthy.

5
00:00:11.788 --> 00:00:16.683
This book is about what happens with them
on every sign-in: who gets access to what,

6
00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:18.167
under which conditions,

7
00:00:18.167 --> 00:00:22.025
and how administrator power is
kept from becoming an open door.

8
00:00:22.025 --> 00:00:24.512
I'll take it through
why, what, who and how.

9
00:00:24.512 --> 00:00:29.723
The short version: in Zero Trust, every
access decision is a policy decision,

10
00:00:29.723 --> 00:00:34.620
and the policy library is the
agency's access control in practice.

11
00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:38.520
Why is every access
decision a policy decision?

12
00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:43.158
Because federal Zero Trust policy says
so, and the platform works that way.

13
00:00:43.158 --> 00:00:47.547
OMB M-22-09 requires that
every access request be

14
00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:51.333
evaluated and that network
location confer no trust.

15
00:00:51.333 --> 00:00:56.282
NIST Special Publication 800-207
describes the architecture:

16
00:00:56.282 --> 00:01:00.795
a policy decision point, separate from
the places where access is enforced.

17
00:01:00.795 --> 00:01:05.245
In Microsoft Entra ID, that decision
point is Conditional Access.

18
00:01:05.245 --> 00:01:09.812
It decides by rule: every applicable
policy must be satisfied,

19
00:01:09.812 --> 00:01:13.483
a block wins, and exclusions
override everything.

20
00:01:13.483 --> 00:01:17.408
What it replaces is access decided
once and trusted afterward.

21
00:01:17.408 --> 00:01:19.857
Inside the network, therefore trusted.

22
00:01:19.857 --> 00:01:22.670
In the group, therefore allowed, forever.

23
00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:25.254
Those assumptions survive
a migration unless

24
00:01:25.254 --> 00:01:27.970
someone deliberately
replaces them with policy.

25
00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:30.258
Two consequences run through the book.

26
00:01:30.258 --> 00:01:35.271
Every exclusion is a standing hole, so
each one needs an owner and a review.

27
00:01:35.271 --> 00:01:39.071
And policy is only as good
as the attributes it reads,

28
00:01:39.071 --> 00:01:41.810
which is why Book 03 matters here.

29
00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:47.085
The same logic applies to administrators,
and this is where most breaches go.

30
00:01:47.085 --> 00:01:50.381
A standing administrator
is always on, at any hour,

31
00:01:50.381 --> 00:01:55.285
so any stolen credential or session
that reaches the role yields all of it.

32
00:01:55.285 --> 00:01:57.514
Stronger authentication helps,

33
00:01:57.514 --> 00:02:01.147
but it doesn't narrow what the
role can do, where, or when.

34
00:02:01.147 --> 00:02:06.270
An eligible administrator is different:
the role is activated only when needed,

35
00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:10.539
for a set time, with approval from
someone other than the requester,

36
00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:13.722
a fresh strong sign-in, and
a log of every elevation.

37
00:02:13.722 --> 00:02:19.441
NSA and CISA rank poor separation of user
and administrator privilege among the most

38
00:02:19.441 --> 00:02:25.002
common misconfigurations they find, and
NIST control AC-6 requires least privilege,

39
00:02:25.002 --> 00:02:28.497
recurring reviews and logged
privileged functions.

40
00:02:28.497 --> 00:02:33.478
There's one standing exception: two
break-glass accounts stay permanently active,

41
00:02:33.478 --> 00:02:36.847
so the tenant can be recovered
if everything else fails.

42
00:02:36.847 --> 00:02:41.311
They're safe only when they're
governed: phishing-resistant, monitored,

43
00:02:41.311 --> 00:02:46.990
tested every ninety days, and documented as
a deviation in the system security plan.

44
00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:50.428
What does the policy
engine actually evaluate?

45
00:02:50.428 --> 00:02:55.056
Signals first: the user, their
groups and roles; the device;

46
00:02:55.056 --> 00:02:59.489
the application; the location;
and sign-in and user risk.

47
00:02:59.489 --> 00:03:03.515
Conditions decide which policies
apply to this particular request.

48
00:03:03.515 --> 00:03:09.166
Then controls: block, or grant with requirements
such as multifactor authentication,

49
00:03:09.166 --> 00:03:12.906
a specific authentication
strength, a compliant device,

50
00:03:12.906 --> 00:03:16.090
an app protection policy,
or acceptance of terms.

51
00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:19.690
And session controls, like
how often to re-authenticate.

52
00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:22.390
Three operating rules
run through the library.

53
00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:25.553
Every policy runs in
report-only mode first,

54
00:03:25.553 --> 00:03:28.715
so its impact is visible
before it enforces.

55
00:03:28.715 --> 00:03:32.278
Policies target personas,
admins, internal staff,

56
00:03:32.278 --> 00:03:35.840
guests and workload
identities, not individuals.

57
00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.604
And deletion isn't final: deleted
policies are recoverable for thirty days,

58
00:03:40.604 --> 00:03:44.215
and a tenant can hold up to
two hundred and forty policies.

59
00:03:44.215 --> 00:03:49.715
One recent platform change matters:
the approved client app grant became

60
00:03:49.715 --> 00:03:56.030
read-only on June 30, 2026, so mobile access
now relies on app protection policies.

61
00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:58.455
What's the structure underneath?

62
00:03:58.455 --> 00:04:01.080
Two parts: privilege and applications.

63
00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:05.041
For privilege: use the least-privileged
built-in role for each task;

64
00:04:05.041 --> 00:04:09.218
keep fewer than five Global
Administrators, as Microsoft recommends;

65
00:04:09.218 --> 00:04:10.875
use role-assignable groups,

66
00:04:10.875 --> 00:04:15.628
which take assigned membership only and
are limited to five hundred per tenant;

67
00:04:15.628 --> 00:04:19.446
and tier everything along
Microsoft's enterprise access model,

68
00:04:19.446 --> 00:04:24.055
which builds on the old Active Directory
tier model rather than replacing it.

69
00:04:24.055 --> 00:04:28.317
For applications: application
owners actually hold administrator

70
00:04:28.317 --> 00:04:32.580
rights over their app, including
adding a credential, so keep them few.

71
00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:35.317
Admin consent goes through
a governed workflow.

72
00:04:35.317 --> 00:04:39.626
Applications authenticate with
certificates or federated credentials

73
00:04:39.626 --> 00:04:43.379
rather than secrets, and Azure
workloads use managed identities.

74
00:04:43.379 --> 00:04:46.750
What people get wrong:
consent grants never expire,

75
00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:50.280
and revoking one leaves
already-issued tokens valid.

76
00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:54.530
So application access needs the
same review discipline as people.

77
00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:57.830
Who decides access, and who executes it?

78
00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:59.580
The book keeps those apart.

79
00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:04.442
Deciding: business and application owners
decide who should have access and why.

80
00:05:04.442 --> 00:05:08.131
The CISO owns the policy
baseline and its exceptions.

81
00:05:08.131 --> 00:05:11.867
And an identity governance office
owns approval chains and reviews.

82
00:05:11.867 --> 00:05:15.655
Executing: identity engineering
builds the policies,

83
00:05:15.655 --> 00:05:18.854
the PIM settings, consent
and access packages.

84
00:05:18.854 --> 00:05:21.793
The help desk handles
assignments under a scoped role.

85
00:05:21.793 --> 00:05:26.480
Security operations handles alerts,
risky sign-ins and response.

86
00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:31.067
Assuring: the ISSO owns the evidence
and findings, the authorizing

87
00:05:31.067 --> 00:05:36.155
official owns accepted risk, and
reviewers recertify access on schedule.

88
00:05:36.155 --> 00:05:37.964
The rule that holds it together:

89
00:05:37.964 --> 00:05:41.305
the people who execute access
changes never approve them.

90
00:05:41.305 --> 00:05:45.856
That's why the help desk works under a
custom role that manages assignments only,

91
00:05:45.856 --> 00:05:49.993
while consent and administrator
roles stay with identity engineering.

92
00:05:49.993 --> 00:05:53.799
The old guide gave the help
desk Application Administrator,

93
00:05:53.799 --> 00:05:58.870
which can grant consent and act as any
app, so that rule existed only on paper.

94
00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:00.233
How.

95
00:06:00.233 --> 00:06:03.544
The policy library is
rolled out in five waves.

96
00:06:03.544 --> 00:06:08.220
Wave one is the foundation, starting
with blocking legacy authentication.

97
00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:12.284
Wave two is administrators:
phishing-resistant authentication

98
00:06:12.284 --> 00:06:15.670
and sign-in only from
privileged access workstations.

99
00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:21.608
Wave three is everyone: an MFA
baseline plus device and app controls.

100
00:06:21.608 --> 00:06:25.758
Wave four is risk: blocking
high-risk users and sign-ins.

101
00:06:25.758 --> 00:06:28.616
And wave five covers
the remaining segments,

102
00:06:28.616 --> 00:06:31.783
guests and workload
identities, and the end state.

103
00:06:31.783 --> 00:06:36.378
Every policy passes the same gates:
two to four weeks in report-only,

104
00:06:36.378 --> 00:06:41.057
then a pilot ring, then expanding
rings, then enforced and monitored.

105
00:06:41.057 --> 00:06:45.332
A wave starts only after the previous
one has run a week without incident.

106
00:06:45.332 --> 00:06:50.695
And rollback means switching a policy off
or back to report-only, never deleting it.

107
00:06:50.695 --> 00:06:53.420
The library is built to CISA's baseline.

108
00:06:53.420 --> 00:07:01.807
It keeps Book 02's policies CA001 to CA017
and adds CA018 to CA038 for the BOD 25-01

109
00:07:01.807 --> 00:07:07.144
Entra ID requirements, including
blocking high-risk users

110
00:07:07.144 --> 00:07:12.400
and requiring phishing-resistant
MFA for all members.

111
00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:15.720
For administrators, Privileged Identity

112
00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:18.950
Management turns privilege
into a governed request.

113
00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:22.900
An administrator holds an eligible
assignment, not an active one.

114
00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:27.031
To use it, they request activation
with a justification and a ticket;

115
00:07:27.031 --> 00:07:31.604
step up through an authentication
context; get approval from someone else;

116
00:07:31.604 --> 00:07:36.325
and receive a time-boxed activation that's
logged, alerted and later reviewed.

117
00:07:36.325 --> 00:07:39.725
The details matter, and the
old guides had several wrong.

118
00:07:39.725 --> 00:07:42.679
Activation can run from
one to twenty-four hours,

119
00:07:42.679 --> 00:07:47.037
and the approval window is a fixed
twenty-four hours with a single stage.

120
00:07:47.037 --> 00:07:52.047
PIM's built-in MFA can be satisfied by
an earlier sign-in in the same session,

121
00:07:52.047 --> 00:07:56.563
so the book uses an authentication
context to force a fresh step-up.

122
00:07:56.563 --> 00:08:00.793
Approvers can't approve their own
requests, and if a role has no approvers

123
00:08:00.793 --> 00:08:05.800
configured, active Privileged Role
Administrators become the approvers by default.

124
00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:09.613
And licensing: PIM needs
Entra ID P2 or ID Governance,

125
00:08:09.613 --> 00:08:14.225
and if those licenses lapse,
eligible assignments are removed.

126
00:08:14.225 --> 00:08:18.545
Appendix B sets every role's
activation time, approvers,

127
00:08:18.545 --> 00:08:25.210
context and notifications, tighter for Tier
0 and Tier 1 than Book 02's first table.

128
00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:29.123
Finally, governance, code and evidence.

129
00:08:29.123 --> 00:08:34.700
Governance: access is requested through
access packages with approval and expiry;

130
00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:40.197
reviews run on a tiered cadence; and the
book reviews groups, not just applications.

131
00:08:40.197 --> 00:08:42.722
Guests have sponsors and are reviewed too.

132
00:08:42.722 --> 00:08:44.577
As code: Conditional Access,

133
00:08:44.577 --> 00:08:48.447
PIM settings and access packages
live in version control.

134
00:08:48.447 --> 00:08:51.347
Changes go through review
rather than the portal,

135
00:08:51.347 --> 00:08:55.518
drift is monitored against a baseline,
and results are exported before

136
00:08:55.518 --> 00:08:59.760
a baseline is changed, because changing
it erases that monitor's history.

137
00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:05.118
Evidence: the book maps all of
this to the access-control,

138
00:09:05.118 --> 00:09:11.291
identification and continuous-monitoring
controls, AC-2, AC-3,

139
00:09:11.291 --> 00:09:17.347
AC-6, IA-2 and CA-7, with every
elevation and every review on record.

140
00:09:17.347 --> 00:09:18.923
Two traps in the tooling.

141
00:09:18.923 --> 00:09:23.240
Reviewing an application doesn't remove
members of the groups assigned to it,

142
00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:25.735
so the groups themselves
have to be reviewed.

143
00:09:25.735 --> 00:09:30.584
And Entra keeps sign-in and audit
logs only thirty days with P1 or P2,

144
00:09:30.584 --> 00:09:34.300
so the evidence has to be
exported from day one.

145
00:09:35.050 --> 00:09:36.275
So, four asks.

146
00:09:36.275 --> 00:09:39.811
Adopt the library: Book
04's Conditional Access

147
00:09:39.811 --> 00:09:43.800
library and wave plan as the
agency's access baseline.

148
00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:48.149
End standing privilege: every
administrator role eligible through PIM,

149
00:09:48.149 --> 00:09:52.425
with two governed break-glass accounts
as the only standing exception.

150
00:09:52.425 --> 00:09:55.623
Separate deciding from
executing: name the owners,

151
00:09:55.623 --> 00:09:59.962
scope the help desk role, and stand
up the identity governance office.

152
00:09:59.962 --> 00:10:02.885
And manage access as
code: version control,

153
00:10:02.885 --> 00:10:07.312
review and drift monitoring, with
the evidence kept from day one.

154
00:10:07.312 --> 00:10:11.612
In Zero Trust, every access
decision is a policy decision.

155
00:10:11.612 --> 00:10:14.081
Writing that policy down, testing it,

156
00:10:14.081 --> 00:10:18.524
and proving it ran is how an agency
knows who can reach what, and why.

157
00:10:18.524 --> 00:10:24.325
Book 04 has the full library and settings,
with every external claim footnoted.

158
00:10:24.325 --> 00:10:28.925
Book 05 covers the device compliance
that Conditional Access reads.

159
00:10:28.925 --> 00:10:29.740
Thank you.
